WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.656
A request path can pass through several
interpreters before a filesystem call.

2
00:00:04.736 --> 00:00:06.348
The HTTP server parses the URL,

3
00:00:06.428 --> 00:00:09.405
the framework may percent-decode route or
query values,

4
00:00:09.485 --> 00:00:11.470
application code may normalize text,

5
00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:14.279
and Node's path module applies platform
path rules.

6
00:00:14.359 --> 00:00:18.763
Security checks must operate on the same
semantic representation used to derive the

7
00:00:18.843 --> 00:00:19.277
target.

8
00:00:19.357 --> 00:00:23.575
Inspect framework documentation and test
actual request handling so code knows

9
00:00:23.655 --> 00:00:26.818
whether a value is still encoded or has
already been decoded.

10
00:00:26.898 --> 00:00:30.061
Do not repeatedly decode a value as a
generic safety measure.

11
00:00:30.141 --> 00:00:33.987
An extra decoding pass can create a
different path after an earlier check,

12
00:00:34.067 --> 00:00:36.486
while another component may decode only
once.

13
00:00:36.566 --> 00:00:38.489
Establish one documented boundary:

14
00:00:38.569 --> 00:00:42.414
obtain the framework's intended decoded
value, reject decoding errors,

15
00:00:42.494 --> 00:00:46.464
validate that representation, and pass
that same value to path resolution.

16
00:00:46.544 --> 00:00:50.885
Avoid maintaining both a checked string
and a differently transformed string that

17
00:00:50.965 --> 00:00:52.268
reaches the filesystem.

18
00:00:52.348 --> 00:00:55.511
For a narrow requirement, an allowlist
grammar is valuable.

19
00:00:55.591 --> 00:00:59.995
An export endpoint that accepts only a
simple logical filename can enforce a bounded

20
00:01:00.075 --> 00:01:04.541
length, known extension, and permitted
character set with no directory components.

21
00:01:04.621 --> 00:01:07.722
That is easier to reason about than
arbitrary nested paths.

22
00:01:07.802 --> 00:01:10.469
Do not generalize the rule into a
traversal filter.

23
00:01:10.549 --> 00:01:14.953
A regular expression for simple names does
not replace root-based containment when

24
00:01:15.033 --> 00:01:17.142
subdirectories are a real requirement.

25
00:01:17.222 --> 00:01:18.896
Keep three decisions distinct.

26
00:01:18.976 --> 00:01:23.318
Filename validation asks whether a logical
name matches the operation's grammar.

27
00:01:23.398 --> 00:01:27.616
Path resolution and containment ask
whether the resulting lexical target stays

28
00:01:27.696 --> 00:01:29.060
within the selected root.

29
00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:33.482
Authorization asks whether the
authenticated subject may access the represented

30
00:01:33.562 --> 00:01:33.996
object.

31
00:01:34.076 --> 00:01:36.185
Unicode normalization, case behavior,

32
00:01:36.265 --> 00:01:39.056
and separator recognition vary across
filesystems,

33
00:01:39.136 --> 00:01:42.982
so avoid home-grown canonicalization that
promises a universal answer.

34
00:01:43.062 --> 00:01:45.543
Use platform-aware APIs, narrow input
models,

35
00:01:45.623 --> 00:01:49.220
and deployment tests to keep every layer
interpreting the same path.
